<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#siege-warfare">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege warfare</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siege warfare is the conduct of military operations to capture or defend fortified places, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, assault, mining or bombardment. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> was known for its siege engineering, and the Babylonian, Chaldean, Hittite and Macedonian empires also relied on it. Later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s artillery made it a master of siege warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-warfare.jpg" alt="Siege warfare" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_orleans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Assyrian army was the strongest and most systematically organised fighting force of its age, and its innovations in tactics, logistics, <b>siege warfare</b> and intelligence influenced warfare in the Near East for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate's basic military weakness was its limited capacity for <b>siege warfare</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Babylon under Nebuchadnezzar operated mainly through annual campaigns in the west, <b>siege warfare</b> and the enforcement of vassalage, rather than through a dense network of garrisoned provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of Rus' showed its skill in <b>siege warfare</b>, and for a century after 1242 punitive expeditions, such as the devastating campaign of 1293 led by Duden, kept the Russian principalities obedient.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hittites conducted systematic <b>siege warfare</b>, with battering rams and siege works described in the Old Hittite account of the siege of Urshu, though their preferred method remained political isolation rather than costly direct assault.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in <b>siege warfare</b> alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
